module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.CanonicalSectionIsoUnique
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ChartTrivialization
import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.EffectiveCartierEtaleEquiv
import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ZeroSchemeIdealSheaf
import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ZeroSchemeLocallyPrincipal

/-!
# An effective Cartier divisor is the zero scheme of its canonical section

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` and let `D ⊆ X` be an effective Cartier divisor.
Its canonical section `1_D` of `𝒪_X(D)` is regular by Stacks 0847 (1), so the zero scheme
`Z(1_D)` of Stacks 0845 is defined; this file proves that it is `D` again, and reads off the
uniqueness of the divisor which Stacks 0847 (2) asserts.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_canonicalSection`: on every
  affine étale chart the evaluation ideal of `(𝒪_X(D), 1_D)` of Stacks 0845 is the defining ideal
  `𝓘_D` of `D`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroScheme_canonicalSection_eq` is the clause of **Stacks 0847 (2)**
  that `D ↦ (𝒪_X(D), 1_D)` followed by `(L, s) ↦ Z(s)` is the identity on effective Cartier
  divisors: `Z(1_D) = D`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.eq_zeroScheme_of_canonicalSectionHom_comp` is the clause of
  **Stacks 0847 (2)** that the divisor is unique: an effective Cartier divisor carrying an
  isomorphism `𝒪_X(D) ≅ L` which sends `1_D` to `s` is the zero scheme `Z(s)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroScheme_eq_of_iso`: the zero scheme of a pair `(L, s)` depends only
  on the isomorphism class of the pair, which is what makes `(L, s) ↦ Z(s)` a map out of those
  isomorphism classes in **Stacks 0847 (3)**.

## Implementation notes

The content is that the evaluation pairing `𝒪_X(D) ⊗ 𝓘_D ≅ 𝒪_X` of Stacks 0841 is *perfect on
the sections over a chart trivializing `𝓘_D`*, and the second description of `𝒪_X(D)` in
Stacks 0841 — as the sheaf-hom `SheafHom_{𝒪_X}(𝓘_D, 𝒪_X)` — is what supplies it.  No comparison
between the sections of a sheafified tensor product and the tensor product of the sections is
needed, and none is available.

Let `V` be a chart with a trivialization `e : 𝒪_X|_V ≅ 𝓘_D|_V`, let `τ` be the generator of
`𝓘_D` over `V` which it exhibits and let `σ₀` be the section of `𝒪_X(D)` over `V` which the
*inverse* trivialization `e⁻¹ : 𝓘_D|_V ⟶ 𝒪_X|_V` is, read through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.associatedInvertibleSheafHomEquiv`.  Contraction with `τ` is a linear
functional `λ_τ` on the sections of `𝒪_X(D)` over `V`, and `λ_τ σ₀ = 1`: by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.associatedInvertibleSheafHomEquiv_apply` the identification evaluates
the pairing as the sheaf-hom does, and `e⁻¹` takes `τ` to the unit section.  It is injective
because a sheaf-hom out of `𝓘_D|_V` is determined by its value on `τ` — every section of `𝓘_D`
below `V` is a multiple of the restriction of `τ`,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_smul_unitIsoGenerator` — so `λ_τ` is bijective and `σ₀` is a
basis with `λ_τ` as its coordinate functional.

`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_eq_span_singleton` then makes the evaluation
ideal over `V` the principal ideal generated by `λ_τ 1_D`, which is `τ` by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.tensorPairing_canonicalSection_tmul`, and `τ` generates `𝓘_D` over `V`.
The reverse containment `𝓘_D ⊆` evaluation ideal needs no trivialization at all, being the same
evaluation of the pairing at the canonical section.  Both sides then spread to an arbitrary
affine chart: `𝓘_D` is a sheaf, so membership descends along a covering by charts which are
affine and trivialize `𝓘_D`, and the evaluation ideal restricts into the evaluation ideal between
affine charts because `𝒪_X(D)` is quasi-coherent.

## Where the rest of Stacks 0847 lives

The remaining clause of part (2) is that the isomorphism `𝒪_X(D) ≅ L` can be chosen to send
`1_D` to `s`; it is proved in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.RegularSectionEffectiveCartier` by matching the two
pairings against `𝓘_D` rather than by cancelling a tensor factor with
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Invertible.isoOfTensorUnit`, which says nothing about the sections it
matches.  With it and with `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroScheme_canonicalSection_eq` the bijection
of part (3) between effective Cartier divisors and pairs `(L, s)` is complete, and that is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.effectiveCartierDivisor_regularSectionPair_mutuallyInverse`.

